# world/winui.list — the conditional Windows GUI world layer
# (docs/win_gui_design.md §2.2/§2.3, sprint WG0). The exact mechanism
# world/cocoaui.list uses: loaded ONLY by a caller that explicitly opts in,
# via `frontend::world::load_list(vm, path)` (CG1, ported and tested on
# Windows), AFTER the base world is up — never from world/world.list, so the
# CLI, the web GUI and the base test suite carry none of it and the base
# world stays byte-identical.
#
# Same format as world.list: one .mst path per line, relative to THIS file's
# directory; blank lines and #-comments skipped. Files reserved 90+ — 63-85
# belong to cocoaui.list and 01-84 to the base world (verify with `ls
# world/`), and starting the Windows layer at 90 leaves both alone.
#
# From WG1 this is what the main-thread UI VM loads at boot, the way
# cocoa_gui/src/boot.rs loads cocoaui.list — `win_gui/src/main.rs` calls
# `VmHandle::load_list` on this file. It is still also loaded by the world
# test run and by anyone driving the probe or the shell from the CLI.
#
# ORDER: numeric, and it has to stay that way for a reason worth stating.
# 90 defines WinProbe, whose bodies name WinArena and WinApi; 91 defines
# those two plus WinShell, whose bodies name WinProbe's WinRef. The
# reference runs BOTH ways, so no ordering of these two files can make
# every name already exist — which is why 90 forward-declares WinArena and
# WinApi with empty bodies and 91 reopens them (WG0's Δ 7, one file
# further). Nothing here executes at load time, so a body compiled against
# an empty class runs only once the real one has landed.
90_winui_probe.mst
91_winui_shell.mst
# WINARM (WG3, docs/sprints/sprint_wg3_detail.md): controls, layout and the
# meaning of a control message. Loaded AFTER 91, and the order is the sprint's
# own: 91 carries the whole flag-and-drain pass with four no-op hooks, and this
# file overrides them. Part 1 of WG3's gate runs with this file making no
# difference at all, which is how "the drain lands before the first control
# exists" gets checked rather than claimed.
92_winui_controls.mst
# WINARM (WG4, docs/ROADMAP.md WG4 row): the shell — view bar, lazy view
# switching, docked transcript, live metrics. Overrides 92's hooks in turn.
93_winui_shell.mst
# WINARM (WG4 D3): the custom-drawn command bar — Fluent glyphs, 9pt labels
# beneath, and the system accent underline on the active view.
94_winui_drawing.mst
# WINARM (WG4 D4): Do It / Print It enablement, tracked by the FOCUSED
# control rather than the active view.
95_winui_focus.mst
# WINARM (WG4 D5): the transcript's splitter — a visible grab bar with a
# chevron and Clear, a draggable dock height, and Cascadia Mono.
96_winui_splitter.mst
# WINARM (WG5a): the Workspace — selection-or-everything, Do It / Print It
# over the primary seam, Ctrl-D / Ctrl-P, and the ghost line.
97_winui_workspace.mst
# WINARM (WG5b-1): the Browser — four panes over the primary's live class
# hierarchy, read-only. Accept and the image path are WG5b-2.
98_winui_browser.mst
# WINARM (WG5b-2): Accept, over `image_store::flows` — the host service
# bindings (`winui_host.dll`, reached through the FFI's new `library:` part),
# the image write path, and the source pane's image read. Loads last because
# its `acceptSource` names WinHost, which this file defines, and reopens
# WinShell to add the verb 93 now routes to.
99_winui_host.mst
# WINARM (WG5c D1/D2): the syntax tokenizer and colour table — both PURE, so
# this file needs nothing from the machine and nothing from the shell. The
# Windows layer has outgrown 90-99 and continues at three digits from 100;
# THIS LIST is the authority on load order, but note a plain `ls` sorts 100_
# before 90_ (`sort -V` puts it right).
100_winui_syntax.mst
# WINARM (WG5c D3): the RichEdit surface. Loads after 95 because it redefines
# `isSourceSurface`, and after 93/98 is irrelevant — they reach it by message
# send at open time, not by name at compile time.
101_winui_richedit.mst
# WINARM (WG5c D4): applying the colour — CHARFORMAT2W derived from winkb,
# EM_SETCHARFORMAT over each run, selection and scroll restored, and the
# whole thing on an edit-idle rather than per keystroke. Loads after 101,
# whose panes it colours, and after 100, whose runs it applies.
102_winui_colour.mst
# WINARM (WG5c D5): the ghost line, re-implemented as an OVERLAY after
# WG5a D4's EM_SETCUEBANNER turned out to be refused (it returns 0 on a
# multiline control). A STATIC raised above the pane, so the hint is never
# part of the document and so can never be evaluated, saved, or lost.
103_winui_ghost.mst
# WINARM (WG6a): the Outliner — one SysTreeView32 over the PRIMARY's own
# browseSnapshot, which already carries the whole Strongtalk outline. No host
# service and no new seam traffic: it renders a tree 98 already caches.
104_winui_outliner.mst
# WINARM (WG6b): Find — Implementors and Senders over the image's own
# queries, and the payoff: a chosen hit JUMPS to the Browser through WG5b-1's
# selection helpers. Loads after 99 (WinHost) and after 98 (the Browser's
# selection helpers it drives).
105_winui_find.mst
# WINARM (WG6c-1): the editor pane — a child of OUR window class, so the door
# is its WndProc and WM_PAINT/WM_KEYDOWN/WM_CHAR all reach Smalltalk. Paints
# `EditorSession`'s rope with WG5c's tokenizer and palette. Loads after 100
# (the tokenizer it names) and after 94 (the drawing helpers it reuses).
106_winui_editorpane.mst
# WINARM (WG6c-2): the editor pane accepts input — WM_CHAR/WM_KEYDOWN to the
# rope's `handleKey:at:`, mouse hit-testing (WG6c-1's arithmetic inverted),
# and undo/redo, which the persistent rope makes free.
107_winui_editorkeys.mst
# WINARM (WG6c-2b): the clipboard. Loads after 107, which dispatches Ctrl-C/X/V
# to it; 91 declares the three as no-ops so 107 is complete without this file.
108_winui_editorclip.mst
# WINARM (WG6c-3): the Editor VIEW — a class picker over the primary's live
# hierarchy, whole-class source from the image, and Save through the shared
# `flows::persist_editor_class`. Loads after 106/107/108 (it positions and
# shows their pane) and after 99, whose host verbs it calls.
109_winui_editorview.mst
# WINARM (WG6d-2): the pane pokes CELLS. `WinRender` over winui_render.dll —
# DirectWrite metrics, a Direct2D/DXGI swapchain — and the WinShell half that
# fills the grid from the tokenizer. Loads LAST because it overrides 106's
# metrics and paint: the renderer is the one authority on a cell's size.
110_winui_render.mst
# WINARM (WG6e): a code pane is an OBJECT. `WinCodePane` holds one pane's
# window, document, caret, selection and renderer attachment — because the
# shell has eight views and three want a code surface AT ONCE, which no
# arrangement of class variables can serve. Loads after 110 (it names
# WinRender) and after 100 (WinSyntax).
111_winui_codepane.mst
# WINARM (WG7-2): the Monitor — every running VM, live. The roster is PUSHED in
# by win_gui on the between-dispatches beat (the exe, because the roster is a
# process-wide static and a DLL would get its own copy), and this renders it.
112_winui_monitor.mst
# WINARM (WG7): writing a file from the shell — the Win32 twin of the Mac's
# `CocoaFile spit:contents:`, and the piece File In / Add to World waited on.
# `WinIo` never shipped: it lives in the TEST file, written to prove the P5
# resolver rather than to be a facility.
113_winui_file.mst
# WINARM (WG7-1): the Debugger — the native face of the VM's HALT loop. The
# primary parks and publishes; this renders. Loads after 110 (the renderer it
# draws on) and 93 (the view it registers into).
114_winui_debugger.mst
# WINARM (WG6c-3, finished in WG7): File In and Add to World. The two verbs
# WG6c-3 named and could not build — File In needs "a fresh world, then your
# file", which is WG7-3's restart, and both need 113's file writer.
115_winui_filein.mst
# WINARM (WG8/SM0): the PIXEL plane — the VM writes video memory rather than
# sending it. Upstream's SM0 in the shape D3D11 allows, on the D3D device
# `winui_render` already owns. Loads after 110, whose renderer holds the plane.
116_winui_pixels.mst

# WINARM (WG8): the cookbook's failure list. `docs/winui-cookbook.md`
# is executed against a live window by `just gate-cookbook`; this is what
# makes the result checkable rather than merely printed.
117_winui_examples.mst

# WINARM (WG9, upstream 6536294 S4): the Tests view. Runs the suite in the
# PRIMARY through the worker seam and renders the result on a cell grid.
# `world/86_sunit_runner.mst` is the far half and loads from world.list.
118_winui_tests.mst

# WINARM (WG10): the gallery. Life on the INDEXED plane, Julia on the direct
# one — the demos that prove WG8 rather than decorate it. Loads after 116,
# whose `drawGalleryOn:` hook it fills in.
119_winui_demos.mst

# WG12: the menu bar — File, World, Demos, View, Help. Built in Smalltalk
# through the same WinApi FFI as everything else and serviced by the same
# flag-and-drain; WM_COMMAND has been allowlisted since WG3.
120_winui_menu.mst

# WG14a: the declarative UI's PURE half — build, normalize, validate, diff.
# No Win32 in it at all, so the whole model is checkable headless before a
# window exists to hide a bug in. Contract adopted from wingui's spec+bind;
# `docs/win_declarative_ui.md` is the decision record.
122_winui_spec.mst

# WG14b: where a node GOES — pure arithmetic over the spec, the space and
# the font metrics, so the realizer that follows is only `create a control
# per rect`. Containers are layout-only here, not windows; see the header.
123_winui_layout.mst

# WG14b: the realizer and the tool window it serves — a spec becomes real
# controls in a second top-level window of the shell's own class, routed by
# the same door. The lifecycle the Mac never abstracted, owned once.
124_winui_toolwin.mst

# WG14b's proof, and the shape every later tool copies: a title, a block that
# answers a spec, a block that handles an event. No window code, no control
# code, no layout code — that is the point. It shows its own patch/rebuild
# counters, so `a click patches and does not rebuild` is checkable by looking.
125_winui_demotool.mst

# WG14c: the SPRITE EDITOR, the first real tool written in the declarative
# UI. The document (44a_spritedoc.mst) is already in the world list on every
# platform; this is only a face, and about a fifth the size of the Cocoa one
# it replaces because the window, the layout and the lifecycle are owned
# elsewhere. Its two surfaces are rgbaPane nodes.
126_winui_spriteed.mst

# WG14c: the Sound Editor migrated onto the declarative UI — the sliders the
# Mac has, without its twelve hand-written mappings: the fields are a table
# and ONE conversion serves every slider. The envelope is a damage-driven
# rgbaPane; only the fields that change the curve repaint it. The WG13 grid
# view (121) remains for now; this is the first-class face.
127_winui_soundtool.mst

# WG14c: HELP, the last of the Mac's four views to reach Windows. Its one
# structural idea is copied exactly — every word lives in the file as
# Smalltalk methods, so the app documents itself from any working directory.
# The content is NOT copied: half the Mac's text describes Cocoa. The reader
# is a `textGrid` pane, the declarative UI's third pane type and the first
# thing to use it.
128_winui_help.mst
